"""Prepared reference solution for the ``true_divide`` operator (MUSA review).

Injected through the project's existing formal override entry point:

    --override true_divide:<this file>:run

Two prepared callsites use this single entrypoint, both of which call the
public export ``flag_gems.true_divide``:

* accuracy  ``tests/test_true_divide.py -m div_tensor``
  (``res_out = flag_gems.true_divide(inp1, inp2)``)
* benchmark ``benchmark/test_true_divide.py -m true_divide``
  (``op_name="true_divide"``, resolved by
  ``Benchmark._candidate_call`` -> ``DynamicOpOverride.get_override``)

The body is exactly the frozen master oracle expression of those cases
(``torch.true_divide(operand, operand)`` on the original same-device
operands), so the declared contract is preserved without reinterpretation:

* out-of-place: a new tensor is returned, no input is mutated, nothing is
  aliased (frozen Definition ``true_divide_tensor``:
  ``effects.mutates == []``, ``returns_alias_of == {}``);
* dtype/device follow ATen type promotion for the real operands, so the
  reviewed ``assert_close`` dtype assertion and the device contract are kept;
* scalar operands degrade to the same ATen scalar overload rather than a
  hand-written formula, so no tolerance/alternative-result logic is added.

This module never imports or calls ``flag_gems`` (the current candidate under
review) and never touches the injected attribute, so there is no dispatch
re-entry: the reviewed accuracy path does not open ``flag_gems.use_gems()``,
and ``Benchmark._candidate_call`` returns a ``nullcontext`` whenever an
override is present. ``torch.true_divide`` is therefore the native ATen
operator in both processes.
"""

import torch


def run(self, other):
    """Elementwise true division (aten::true_divide.Tensor), out-of-place."""
    return torch.true_divide(self, other)
